WEBVTT


00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:10.400
machine knitters often use scrap yarn or
waste yarn to start and end our knitting.

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:16.880
By scrapping on, we can hang weight in the
scrap yarn to make starting the garment

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.270
easier and to protect
delicate garment yarn.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:27.140
We can scrap on both
open and close cast ons.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.580
For both techniques.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.520
Start with a yarn that's similar
in weight to your garment

00:00:32.520 --> 00:00:32.910
yarn.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.670
Use any cast on method you choose.

00:00:36.390 --> 00:00:41.430
I've used a simple e-wrap cast
on knit, a few Row of scrap.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:49.430
And then knit one row of Ravel cord.

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:54.290
The ravel cord should be a smooth,
strong yarn of contrast in color.

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:23.735
For a closed cast on, use your garment
yarn and cast on over the ravel cord

00:01:23.745 --> 00:01:24.095
stitches.

00:01:31.155 --> 00:01:32.115
Continue knitting.

00:01:34.575 --> 00:01:39.705
Many knitters will keep the scrap on the
work and it will help shape the piece

00:01:39.705 --> 00:01:40.695
while you're blocking.

00:01:40.935 --> 00:01:45.164
So don't take it off too quickly, but
this is a matter of personal preference.